          %title Phase space
          %h2#PhaseSpace Phase space
          %p In this chapter we discuss these topics:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#PreymplecticPhaseSpace"} Covariant phase space
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#BVResolutionOfTheCovariantPhaseSpace"} BV-Resolution of the covariant phase space
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#HamiltonianLocalObservablesOnACauchySurface"} Hamiltonian local observables
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p
            It might seem that with the construction of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+observables"} local observables
            (def.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LocalObservables"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/on-shell"} on-shell
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+field+histories"} space of field histories
            (prop.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#EulerLagrangeOperatorForTivialVectorBundleOverMinkowskiSpacetime"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+theory"} field theory
            defined by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+density"} Lagrangian density
            (def.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LocalLagrangianDensityOnSecondOrderJetBundleOfTrivialVectorBundleOverMinkowskiSpacetime"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+histories"} field histories
            are realized, and which
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable"} observable
            properties these have.
          %p
            In particular, if the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler-Lagrange+equation"} Euler-Lagrange
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            (def.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#EulerLagrangeEquationsOnTrivialVectorFieldBundleOverMinkowskiSpacetime"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+surfaces"} Cauchy surfaces
            (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CauchySurface"}
            below), i.e. spatial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codimension"} codimension
            1 slices of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetimes"} spacetimes
            such that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+history"} field history
            is uniquely specified already by its restriction to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+neighbourhood"} infinitesimal neighbourhood
            of that spatial slice, then a sufficiently complete collection of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+observables"} local observables
            whose spacetime support (def.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SpacetimeSupport"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covering"} covers
            that Cauchy surface allows to
            %em predict
            the evolution of the field histories through time from that Cauchy surface.
          %p
            This is all what one might think a theory of physical fields should accomplish, and in fact this is essentially all that was thought to be required of a theory of nature from about
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Isaac+Newton"} Isaac Newton
            = succeed "\u2019s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Max+Planck"} Max Planck
          %p
            But we have seen that a remarkable aspect of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+field+theory"} Lagrangian field theory
            is that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+differential"} de Rham differential
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+Lagrangian+density"} local Lagrangian density
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{L}
            (def.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LocalLagrangianDensityOnSecondOrderJetBundleOfTrivialVectorBundleOverMinkowskiSpacetime"}
            %em two
            kinds of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variational+differential+forms"} variational differential forms
            (prop.
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#EulerLagrangeOperatorForTivialVectorBundleOverMinkowskiSpacetime"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler-Lagrange+form"} Euler-Lagrange form
            which determines the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            = succeed "." do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:EulerLagrangeEquationGeneral"} (?)
          %p
            However, there is a second contribution: The
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presymplectic+current"} presymplectic current
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi BFV
                  %mo ∈
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi Σ
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega_{BFV} \in \Omega^{p,2}_{\Sigma}(E)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:PresymplecticCurrent"} (?)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transgression+of+variational+differential+forms"} transgression
            (def.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TransgressionOfVariationalDifferentialFormsToConfigrationSpaces"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+field+histories"} space of field histories
            restricted to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/submanifolds"} submanifolds
            of dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            (i.e. of spacetime “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codimension"} codimension
            1”). There may be such submanifolds such that this restriction to their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+neighbourhood"} infinitesimal neighbourhood
            (example
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#InfinitesimalNeighbourhood"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/on-shell"} on-shell
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+field+histories"} space of field histories
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+surfaces"} Cauchy surfaces
            (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CauchySurface"}
            below).
          %p
            By the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+Noether+theorem"} Hamiltonian Noether theorem
            (prop.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HamiltonianDifferentialForms"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presymplectic+current"} presymplectic current
            induces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+symmetries"} infinitesimal symmetries
            acting on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+histories"} field histories
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+observables"} local observables
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket+Lie+n-algebra"} local Poisson bracket
            (prop.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LocalPoissonBracket"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transgression+of+variational+differential+forms"} transgression
            (def.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TransgressionOfVariationalDifferentialFormsToConfigrationSpaces"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presymplectic+current"} presymplectic current
            to these
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+surfaces"} Cauchy surfaces
            yields the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+symmetry"} infinitesimal symmetry
            group acting on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/on-shell"} on-shell
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+histories"} field histories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+bracket"} Lie bracket
            is the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            pairing on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/on-shell"} on-shell
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
            (example
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#EvaluationOfTransgressedVariationalFormsOnTangentVectorsForFreeFieldTheory"}
            below). This data, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/on-shell"} on-shell
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+field+histories"} space of field histories
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+neighbourhood"} infinitesimal neighbourhood
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+surface"} Cauchy surface
            equipped with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+symmetry"} infinitesimal symmetry
            exhibited by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            is called the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            of the theory (def.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PhaseSpaceAssociatedWithCauchySurface"}
          %p
            In fact if enough
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+surfaces"} Cauchy surfaces
            exist, then the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presymplectic+forms"} presymplectic forms
            associated with any one choice turn out do agree after
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback+of+differential+forms"} pullback
            to the full
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/on-shell"} on-shell
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+field+histories"} space of field histories
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+phase+space"} covariant phase space
            of the theory (prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CovariantPhaseSpace"}
            below) which is hence manifestly independent of aa choice of space/time splitting. Accordingly, also the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/on-shell"} on-shell
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
            exists in a covariant form; for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theories"} free field theories
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Green+hyperbolic+differential+equation"} Green hyperbolic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            (def.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#GreenHyperbolicDifferentialOperator"}
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peierls-Poisson+bracket"} Peierls-Poisson bracket
            (theorem
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PPeierlsBracket"}
            below). The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+kernel"} integral kernel
            for this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peierls-Poisson+bracket"} Peierls-Poisson bracket
            is called the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+propagator"} causal propagator
            (prop.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#GreenFunctionsAreContinuous"}
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal+ordered+product"} normal ordered
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+real+number"} positive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frequency"} frequency
            component”, called the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wightman+propagator"} Wightman propagator
            (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#StandardHadamardDistributionOnMinkowskiSpacetime"}
            below) as well as the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-ordered+product"} time-ordered
            variant, called the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+propagator"} Feynman propagator
            (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FeynmanPropagatorForKleinGordonEquationOnMinkowskiSpacetime"}
            below), which we discuss in detail in
            %em
              %a{:href => "#Propagators"} Propagators
            below, control the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            for constructing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+quantization"} deforming
            the commutative pointwise product of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/on-shell"} on-shell
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
            to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-commutative+algebra"} non-commutative product
            governed to first order by the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peierls-Poisson+bracket"} Peierls-Poisson bracket
          %p
            To see how such a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+quantization"} deformation quantization
            comes about conceptually from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            strucure, notice from the basic principles of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            that given any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            which is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant"} invariant
            with respect to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetry+group"} symmetry group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} acting
            on the space (here: the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presymplectic+current"} presymplectic current
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+quotient"} homotopy quotient
            of that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            by that
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetry+group"} symmetry group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+quotient"} homotopy quotient
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+vector+field"} infinitesimal symmetries of the presymplectic current
            is called the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+groupoid"} symplectic groupoid
            and that the
            %em true
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+observables"} algebra of observables
            is hence the (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polarization"}> polarized
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid+convolution+algebra"} convolution algebra of functions
            on this groupoid. This turns out to the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+quantum+observables"}> algebra of quantum observables
            ” and the passage from the naive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+observables"} local observables
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presymplectic+manifold"} presymplectic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            to this non-commutative algebra of functions on its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+quotient"} homotopy quotient
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+groupoid"} symplectic groupoid
            is called
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "#Quantization"} below
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+phase+space"} covariant phase space
            is the crucial construction to be extracted from a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+field+theory"} Lagrangian field theory
          %p
